.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt SYSINFO 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysinfo
.Nd what this machine is serving, and what it has left
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints five lines, each a live answer from the kernel or the file server
at the moment it was asked; nothing comes from a log and nothing is
estimated:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
release: dev (kernel_abi 1, fsd_protocol 2, fs_format 2, app_abi 1)
boot: 7c0e2b6a9d4f41e3a2b5c8d9e0f1a2b3, up 0 days 00:12:34 (754434 ticks)
memory: 118540 KiB free, 12532 KiB allocated (29635 + 3133 frames of 4096 bytes)
kernel: 6 process slots, 41 handles, 14 pipes, 2 cells
filesystem: absd:0123456789abcdef, 16216 KiB, 9960 KiB used (62%), 6256 KiB free, commit 212
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width "filesystem"
.It release
the release the file server serves, with the protocol and format it
answers; the two ABI generations are the ones this command was built
for, which the runtime found the kernel to match
.Pq Xr release 1 .
.It boot
the kernel's boot identity and the boot tick counter, as
.Xr uptime 1
prints them.
.It memory
the kernel's frame allocator: frames free and frames allocated, each
4096 bytes, among the frames the allocator tracks (usable memory below
256 MiB, less what the boot reserved), for every process and the kernel
itself.
Their sum is not the machine's memory.
There is no other memory accounting; a process's own heap window is not
shown.
When the kernel's exhaustion test has set an allocation limit, it is
printed too.
.It kernel
the kernel's object census: process slots in use (a slot stays in use
from creation until the process is reaped, so an exited process not yet
waited for counts), handles, pipes, and cells, across the whole system.
The counts include
.Nm
itself.
.It filesystem
the filesystem behind every session, as
.Nm df
reports it: identity, data blocks in all, in use, and free, and the
commit sequence durable on the disk.
.El
.Pp
Every number is for the whole machine.
Nothing names another session or another process; the census is counts
only.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when every line was answered; 1 when a source refused, in which case
that line says so in place and the rest are still printed; 2 when given
any argument.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A process without a file server channel
.Pq Ql nofs sysinfo
prints the kernel lines, the release line as
.Ql no file server serves this process ,
and
.Ql filesystem: no file server channel ,
with status 1.
A file server that does not answer when the command starts is the
runtime's refusal, status 104, before anything is printed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr release 1 ,
.Xr uptime 1 ,
